    "title": "Cleaning Business Monthly Expenses: Build a Job-Based Budget",
    "seoTitle": "Cleaning Monthly Expenses: Payroll and Jobs",
    "description": "Build a monthly cleaning business budget that separates job costs from overhead, owner pay and cash timing, then calculates required recurring jobs.",
    "metaDescription": "Budget cleaning monthly expenses with paid labor, job costs and fixed overhead. Reconcile payroll against productive hours and test lost bookings.",
    "image": "/images/blog/cleaning-business-monthly-expenses-editorial.webp",
    "alt": "Cleaning business owner reviews expense records beside supplies, a calculator and vehicle keys",
    "calculatorSlug": "cleaning-business-break-even-calculator",
    "opening": "A cleaning business does not have one useful monthly expense number. A business with five deep cleans and a business with forty recurring visits can spend the same on insurance but very different amounts on labor, supplies and fuel. Budget the cost of each completed job separately from bills you owe even when nobody books.",
    "unit": "jobs",
